Model:
To determine when equilibrium (steady state) is reached, we need to use the formula for the **half-life** of a drug being prescribed at a **constant dose**:

$$
\text{Time to reach steady state} = \frac{\text{Half-life}}{\text{Dose per day}} = \frac{7 \, \text{days}}{0.1 \, \text{mg/day}} = 70 \, \text{days}
$$

However, this formula applies to **continuous infusion** and **steady-state** dosing where the drug is administered at a constant rate. In this case, the **maintenance dose is 0.1 mg/day**, and the **half-life is 7 days**, so the time to reach steady state would be:

$$
\boxed{\text{E}} \, \text{days}
$$

**Answer:** (E) \pu{7 days}
